
🔹 What is LinkedHashMap?
	•	LinkedHashMap<K,V> is a subclass of HashMap.
	•	It stores key–value pairs like a normal HashMap.
	•	BUT unlike HashMap, it maintains a linked list of entries in the order they were inserted (or access order if configured).
	•	That means iteration order is predictable.

⸻

🔹 Internal Working
	•	Internally, it uses a hash table (like HashMap) + a doubly linked list connecting all entries.
	•	Each entry knows:
	•	its key
	•	its value
	•	pointer to next in bucket (for hashing)
	•	pointers to before and after entries (for maintaining order)

⸻

🔹 Types of Ordering
	1.	Insertion Order (default)
	•	Iteration happens in the same order you inserted keys.
	2.	Access Order (if enabled)
	•	If you pass true in the constructor, the iteration order is based on last access (like LRU caches).


⸻

🔹 Features
	1.	Preserves order (insertion or access).
	2.	Allows null key (only one) and multiple null values (like HashMap).
	3.	Iteration is predictable (useful in caches, ordered maps).
	4.	Performance almost same as HashMap: O(1) for get/put, but with a little extra memory for linked list pointers.

⸻

🔹 When to Use?
	•	When you want fast lookups like HashMap, but also need a consistent iteration order.
	•	Example:
	•	Building caches (access-order mode).
	•	Maintaining insertion order of user inputs.
	•	Implementing LRU eviction policies.
